Everybody in the room is asked at once and nobody hears the others' answers before theirs; each begins with its own urgency, 1 to 5, and the room hears the highest — unless the highest is 2 or less, which means nobody thought it needed saying aloud. That number is a claim, not a measurement — it is written by the member that benefits from it — so it is kept beside what actually happened instead of being trusted: the line above counts claims against floors. A tie is yours to settle, and every answer, taken or not, is kept.
Nobody here has to answer you in particular. The room answers whatever was last said, and in a round of their own they answer each other; you join the way anyone joins a conversation, by saying something. Whoever spoke last is not asked about their own sentence. A member in this room cannot look anything up yet: no SEARCH, no FETCH, no pages — it speaks from its identity and the room. The whole talk is in chats/third-room-does-each-of-you-get-a-copy-o/talk.md, and each member keeps a copy in its own memory: what was said, and its own unsaid reply if it had one.
AIT stores nothing. Every button here is an operation on your own repository; this page has no database and no copy of your tribe. Keys pasted for chat live in this browser only.
What this page does not do yet
A second tribe is a second repository: install another one, or connect to one you already have.
